{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pulls/2636","id":3571369946,"node_id":"PR_kwDOM2nIG87U3sfa","html_url":"https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pull/2636","diff_url":"https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pull/2636.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pull/2636.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/issues/2636","commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pulls/2636/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pulls/2636/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/issues/2636/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/statuses/e56a786a822ce13ae1e4fdc53c027630c7029c76","number":2636,"state":"open","locked":false,"title":"SEP-2636: Progressive Tool Disclosure","user":{"login":"SylonZero","id":4340182,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQzNDAxODI=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4340182?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero","html_url":"https://github.com/SylonZero","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"## Summary\r\n\r\nThis SEP introduces **Progressive Tool Disclosure**: a two-stage discovery lifecycle that decouples the lightweight catalog payload returned to a model from the full invocation contract required to call a tool.\r\n\r\nTwo new methods are added. `tools/catalog` returns a compact `ToolCatalogEntry` per tool — name, one-line summary, tags, optional annotations, and a content hash of the underlying schema. `tools/describe` returns the complete `Tool` (including `inputSchema` and `outputSchema`) for an explicitly named subset, batched into a single round trip. A new server capability, `tools.progressiveDisclosure`, gates both methods. The existing `tools/list` method is unchanged and remains the backward-compatible floor.\r\n\r\nThe `tools/catalog` method also accepts an optional opaque `query` string with semantics drawn from [SEP-1821](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/issues/1821), letting agents perform a `query → describe → call` flow that loads full JSON Schemas only for the small set of tools actually being considered for invocation.\r\n\r\n## Motivation and Context\r\n\r\nThe current `tools/list` method returns a monolithic `Tool` record per available tool — name, description, full `inputSchema`, optional `outputSchema`, annotations. For real-world MCP servers, especially those wrapping enterprise applications, these records are large: 300–2000 tokens per tool is typical, dominated by JSON Schema for parameters with rich enums, format hints, and nested object shapes. This entire payload is materialised in the LLM's context on every turn, before the model has even decided whether tools are relevant.\r\n\r\nThe community has converged on the symptom from several angles, but without addressing the structural issue:\r\n\r\n- [SEP-1821](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/issues/1821) (Dynamic Tool Discovery) adds a `query` parameter on `tools/list`. Reduces *how many* records are returned; each matching record is still full-schema.\r\n- [SEP-1862](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pull/1862) (Tool Resolution) adds per-call annotation refinement. Orthogonal to upfront catalog cost.\r\n- [SEP-1881](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/issues/1881) (Scope-Filtered Discovery) standardises auth-driven filtering. A user with full scopes legitimately holding 200 tools is not helped.\r\n- [Issue #2470](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/issues/2470) (Capability-Aware Tool Presentation) proposes per-tool tier hints. Reduces individual record size; catalog enumeration model unchanged.\r\n- [SEP-2564](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pull/2564) (Server-Side Filtering for List Methods) adds glob-based name filters. Pattern matching only; matching records are still full-schema.\r\n\r\nWhat's missing across all of these is the structural change: **separating the discovery contract (what tools exist, roughly what they do) from the invocation contract (the exact schema needed to call them).** Once those are separate, every other proposal in this space composes more cleanly: search filters operate on the cheap surface, scope filtering hides records at the catalog layer, capability tiers describe how the catalog summary is generated, and tool resolution refines annotations after `describe`.\r\n\r\n## What This SEP Proposes\r\n\r\n- New method **`tools/catalog`** returning `ToolCatalogEntry[]` — name, required `summary` (≤200 chars), optional `tags`, required `schemaHash` (SHA-256 over RFC 8785 canonical JSON of the full `Tool`), optional lightweight `annotations`. Accepts optional opaque `query` param.\r\n- New method **`tools/describe`** returning full `Tool[]` for an explicitly named subset, batched. Returns `-32002 RESOURCE_NOT_AVAILABLE` with `data.unknownNames` for unknown or unauthorized names (distinct from `-32602 Invalid params`).\r\n- New server capability **`tools.progressiveDisclosure: boolean`**.\r\n- **`tools/list` unchanged**, full backward compatibility. A trivial polyfill derives the new methods from any existing `tools/list`-implementing server.\r\n- **Two distinct change domains** distinguished by hash stability: invocation-contract changes (hash changes, cached schemas MUST be invalidated) vs. discovery-metadata changes (hash unchanged, `summary`/`tags` may have changed, caches remain valid).\r\n\r\n## How Has This Been Tested?\r\n\r\nA complete reference prototype has been published as a self-contained workspace package on a branch of the official TypeScript SDK fork:\r\n\r\n- **Fork branch**: [`SylonZero/typescript-sdk@sep/progressive-tool-disclosure`](https://github.com/SylonZero/typescript-sdk/tree/sep/progressive-tool-disclosure/examples/progressive-disclosure)\r\n\r\nPer the TypeScript SDK's `CONTRIBUTING.md` policy that spec-touching changes require a SEP first, the prototype is **intentionally not wired into the SDK's internal `Server`/`Client` request-handler registry yet.** It lives as a self-contained workspace package under `examples/`, importing nothing from the SDK and imported by nothing in the SDK. Integration follows the existing experimental pattern (e.g., SEP-1686 Tasks) and is deferred until this SEP is accepted.\r\n\r\nWhat the prototype does include:\r\n\r\n- **Implementation** (`src/`, 7 files, ~660 lines of TypeScript): wire types, RFC 8785 canonical JSON, SHA-256 schema hash, `ProgressiveDisclosureServer` polyfill with pagination + query matching + batch limits, `listToolsCatalog` / `describeTools` client helpers, and `ProgressiveDisclosureCache` implementing the recommended hit/miss/reconcile pattern with an optional persistence hook.\r\n- **Tests** (`tests/`, 4 files, **35 passing** vitest tests): canonical JSON correctness, schema hash stability, polyfill behaviour (pagination, query matching, batch limits, unknown name handling with `-32002`), client cache (cold cache, partial cache, reconcile on hash change, persistence, namespace isolation). Strict TypeScript clean (`noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`).\r\n- **Benchmark** (`bench/`): 37 synthetic tools modelled on the MindStaq MCP service; primary tokenizer is `cl100k_base` via `js-tiktoken` (the GPT-3.5/4/4o encoding; defensible proxy for Anthropic on JSON payloads). Two heuristic estimators retained for sanity-check comparison.\r\n- **Documentation** (`SPEC.md`, `INTEGRATION.md`, `API.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `README.md`): the branch is self-contained for reviewers. `INTEGRATION.md` is a step-by-step server-side + client-side adoption guide; `API.md` is a one-page reference of every public export.\r\n\r\nTo reproduce:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit clone https://github.com/SylonZero/typescript-sdk.git\r\ncd typescript-sdk && git checkout sep/progressive-tool-disclosure\r\ncd examples/progressive-disclosure\r\nnpm install\r\nnpm run typecheck   # strict TS clean\r\nnpm test            # 35/35 pass\r\nnpm run bench       # produces bench/results.md\r\nnpm run demo        # in-process lifecycle: cold cache → 1 describe; warm → 0; reconcile → evict\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Measured performance\r\n\r\nFrom the included 37-tool synthetic catalog, `cl100k_base`:\r\n\r\n| Scenario | Tokens | Savings vs baseline |\r\n|---|---:|---:|\r\n| Baseline `tools/list` (n=37) | 5,238 | — |\r\n| `tools/catalog` only | 2,919 | **44.3%** |\r\n| `tools/catalog` + `tools/describe(k=1)` | 3,223 | 38.5% |\r\n| `tools/catalog` + `tools/describe(k=3)` (typical turn) | 3,458 | **34.0%** |\r\n| `tools/catalog` + `tools/describe(k=10)` | 4,732 | 9.7% |\r\n| Steady state (cache hit, catalog only) | 2,919 | **44.3%** |\r\n\r\nThe curve shape is the meaningful artifact: largest win at small k with cache warm (the common case), gracefully degrading toward baseline as the per-turn pick count grows. §Performance Implications in the SEP discusses this honestly — the SEP's claim is meaningful reduction at the typical operating point, not universally-free savings.\r\n\r\n## Relationship to Other SEPs\r\n\r\nThis proposal is deliberately composable with the related in-flight work:\r\n\r\n- **SEP-1821 (Dynamic Tool Discovery, @truehazker)**: This SEP hosts the preferred query-bearing discovery surface for token-efficient catalogs on `tools/catalog`. It does not propose deprecating `query` on `tools/list`; the two surfaces target different cost regimes. Long-term convergence is a question for the community to answer in concert with SEP-1821's authors. A friendly co-authorship invitation is planned once a sponsor is found.\r\n- **SEP-1862 (Tool Resolution, @nickcoai)**: Fully orthogonal. `tools/resolve` operates on the full `Tool` returned by `tools/describe`.\r\n- **SEP-1881 (Scope-Filtered Tool Discovery)**: Fully orthogonal. Scope filtering applies at the catalog layer; this SEP lets that filtering manifest through a cheaper surface.\r\n- **Issue #2470 (Capability-Aware Tool Presentation)**: Complementary. The `summary` field is a natural place for a tier-aware short description.\r\n- **SEP-2564 (Server-Side Filtering for List Methods)**: Compatible. Glob `filter` parameters could be added to `tools/catalog` in a follow-up without disturbing the structure here.\r\n\r\n## Breaking Changes\r\n\r\n**None.** This SEP is fully backward-compatible.\r\n\r\n- Servers that do not advertise `tools.progressiveDisclosure` are unaffected; clients MUST NOT issue the new methods against them.\r\n- Clients that do not implement progressive disclosure continue using `tools/list` unchanged.\r\n- Servers advertising the capability MUST continue to support `tools/list` for older clients.\r\n- The polyfill in the reference prototype lets SDK authors light up the capability for any server that already implements `tools/list`, with zero application-code changes.\r\n\r\nExisting types (`Tool`, `ToolAnnotations`, `ServerCapabilities.tools`) are extended additively only.\r\n\r\n## Types of changes\r\n\r\n- [ ] Bug fix (non-breaking change which fixes an issue)\r\n- [x] New feature (non-breaking change which adds functionality)\r\n- [ ] Breaking change (fix or feature that would cause existing functionality to change)\r\n- [x] Documentation update *(new SEP file under `seps/`)*\r\n\r\n## Checklist\r\n\r\n- [x] I have read the [MCP Documentation](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) and the [SEP guidelines](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/community/sep-guidelines)\r\n- [x] I have reviewed the [MCP design principles](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/community/design-principles) — this proposal is deliberately additive, minimal, and composable with existing proposals in the space\r\n- [x] I have explored the problem space and validated that others share the problem (see §Motivation's survey of SEP-1821 / 1862 / 1881 / 2564 / Issue #2470)\r\n- [x] I have built a prototype that demonstrates the solution (linked above; 35/35 tests passing, reproducible benchmark numbers)\r\n- [x] The SEP was written *after* the prototype, informed by what the prototype taught us (two rename cycles happened because of implementation experience: `tools/index` → `tools/catalog` after sponsor feedback anticipation, and `-32602` → `-32002` after modelling authorization parity; both documented in `examples/progressive-disclosure/CHANGELOG.md`)\r\n- [x] Appropriate error handling (`-32002 RESOURCE_NOT_AVAILABLE` with `data.unknownNames`; authorization parity between missing and unauthorized)\r\n- [x] Documentation suitable for reviewers is included (SEP itself + SPEC/INTEGRATION/API/CHANGELOG on the prototype branch)\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\n### Sponsor\r\n\r\nSeeking a sponsor. Tagging @davidsp (who sponsored SEP-1850 and has context on the SEP-process mechanics) for initial consideration — no pressure either way, and I'm happy to re-route if someone else is a better fit.\r\n\r\n### Post-merge mechanics\r\n\r\nPer repo convention for SEP files:\r\n\r\n- The SEP file will be renamed from `seps/0000-progressive-tool-disclosure.md` to `seps/{PR-number}-progressive-tool-disclosure.md` in a follow-up commit on this branch once the PR number is assigned.\r\n- Three `SEP-XXXX` occurrences will be updated to `SEP-{PR-number}` at the same time: the SEP title, the Abstract reference, and line 4 of `src/types.ts` on the prototype branch.\r\n\r\n### Community practice note\r\n\r\nThe CONTRIBUTING.md summary for specification proposals is *\"explore the problem space and validate that others share the problem, build a prototype that demonstrates a solution, then write the SEP based on what the prototype taught you.\"* That's the sequence this work followed. The prototype taught us two things worth calling out:\r\n\r\n1. **`summary` quality is the single highest-leverage adoption lever.** An auto-generated summary (first sentence of `description`, truncated) is acceptable; a curated one-liner is strictly better. The SEP makes `summary` REQUIRED but permits both.\r\n2. **The `cache`-reconcile pattern pays off only with per-tool hashes, not the existing `list_changed` notification alone.** Per-tool SHA-256 turns invalidation into a set-difference operation rather than a full refetch. This is why `schemaHash` is on the wire.\r\n\r\n## AI Assistance Disclosure\r\n\r\nPer the [CONTRIBUTING.md AI Contributions policy](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md#ai-contributions):\r\n\r\n> This SEP, its reference prototype, and this PR description were drafted with substantial assistance from **Claude Opus 4.7** (Anthropic), used as a pair-programmer and writing collaborator throughout.\r\n\r\n**Extent of AI use:**\r\n\r\n- **SEP document**: Drafted collaboratively with Claude Opus 4.7 over multiple iterations. Structure, arguments, and tradeoffs were shaped by back-and-forth; the author (@SylonZero) reviewed and edited every section and owns the final technical positions.\r\n- **Reference prototype code**: Written collaboratively with Claude Opus 4.7 — types, polyfill, client cache, benchmark, and all 35 tests. The author reviewed the code, ran the tests, verified benchmark reproducibility with `cl100k_base`, and takes responsibility for correctness.\r\n- **Review/comment responses on this PR going forward**: I will author responses myself; if any individual response is drafted with AI assistance, I will disclose that in the reply.\r\n\r\nThe author personally understands every claim, line of code, and tradeoff in this proposal, can defend each one in review, and is the responsible human on the other end of the conversation.","labels":[],"milestone":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"created_at":"2026-04-23T06:10:09Z","updated_at":"2026-06-21T06:38:37Z","closed_at":null,"merged_at":null,"merge_commit_sha":"c14a3e6d5ea7a6aea3a2afa47a0d41f7a0336514","assignee":null,"assignees":[],"requested_reviewers":[],"requested_teams":[],"head":{"label":"SylonZero:sep/progressive-tool-disclosure","ref":"sep/progressive-tool-disclosure","repo":{"id":1218679288,"node_id":"R_kgDOSKOR-A","name":"modelcontextprotocol","full_name":"SylonZero/modelcontextprotocol","owner":{"login":"SylonZero","id":4340182,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQzNDAxODI=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4340182?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero","html_url":"https://github.com/SylonZero","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SylonZero/repos",